    "69b2db231a38f4046de4bcafd547c0279d208424": {
      "message": "refactor(comfy): report what the install has instead of grading a fixed catalog\n\nThe first version carried an 83-entry `capabilities.json` that declared what\nComfyUI can do and graded the machine against it. That answered a question\nnobody asked and cost real maintenance: every entry duplicated knowledge that\nthe install itself already exposes, and adding a model family meant editing a\ndata file before the tool could see it.\n\nThis replaces the declaration with inspection. The tool now reports what is on\nthis machine and what that adds up to:\n\n- `classify`: guesses a weight's family from its folder and file name (SDXL,\n  WAN video, VAE, upscaler, LoRA, …) and buckets node classes by purpose. A file\n  that matches nothing stays unclassified rather than being forced into a family.\n- `pipelines`: ten common pipelines, each judged by the weights and node classes\n  found, with the evidence printed next to the verdict. States are ready /\n  needs weights / needs nodes / needs both / unknown — `unknown` is what an\n  offline scan reports for anything only its nodes could decide.\n- `report`: environment, weights by folder with sizes, custom nodes, node\n  buckets, then the pipeline table. `doctor`, `models` and `nodes` stay as the\n  follow-up questions. `--json` and `--md` carry the same facts.\n\nThe install is never written to, and the tool still decides everything from one\nprobe: no stored claims, no download, no process control.\n\nRemoved: capabilities.json, lib/grade.mjs, the catalog schema test and its\nfixture. Tests: 29 cases, including the fake ComfyUI and the offline scan.",
