    "b2ecc649927f3bf37c89cceade57b50383a458c1": {
      "message": "feat(comfy): add a capability catalog and a local availability probe\n\nTwo questions, one data file: ComfyUI can do a lot, but which of it works on\nthis machine right now?\n\n`capabilities.json` lists 83 capabilities across eight families (text-to-image,\nediting, vision utilities, video, audio, 3D, LLM nodes, engine), each with the\nnode classes and weight files it needs, plus memory and disk thresholds and the\ncustom node worth installing. The file is machine-independent on purpose: it is\nthe catalogue of what ComfyUI can do, not what one laptop can.\n\n`comfy.mjs` grades that catalogue against one installation on two axes that are\nnever collapsed: install (ready / partial / blocked / unknown) and machine\n(recommended / tight / too big). It reads a running server through\n`/system_stats`, `/object_info` and `/models`, and falls back to scanning\n`models/`, `custom_nodes/` and `comfyui_version.py` when the server is down —\nreporting node-dependent entries as unknown instead of guessing. `--plan\n--budget` turns the grades into a download order: one step installs everything\na capability still lacks, ranked by the value of what it turns on.\n\n- probe: online inventory with an `/object_info` fallback, offline directory\n  scan, machine facts (chip, memory, free disk via df)\n- grade: two-axis verdicts, missing dependency detail, greedy plan with a\n  subset coverage test for planned requirements\n- cli: capabilities / doctor / models / nodes, table, JSON and Markdown output,\n  exit codes documented\n- tests: 29 cases with a fake ComfyUI and an offline directory fixture\n- docs: bilingual README, generated report for this machine\n\nThis is deliberately not a DSH plugin yet: a CLI is enough for an agent to run\nmid-conversation, and packaging can move `lib/` unchanged once the catalogue\nsettles.",
